if you ever heard of unravel then it's  almost certain that you know about the  game's hero he's a wool wrap doll called  Yanni who as far as heroes go is just  delightful he's an adorable silent  quaint little creature Yanni is about as  big as a kitten but he seems far more  fragile because he's made only of cable  wire and yarn because he's so flimsy  he's scared of pretty much everything he  jumps when he's approached by a  butterfly he's absolutely terrified of  cars when he's crossing the road his  eyes which are two big white dots of  wool are just wide enough to seem like  he's permanently startled he doesn't  have any of our facial features as my  mouth no eyebrows and the cheekbones and  yet he's animated so wonderfully that  you can get a real sense of his  character you can tell he's pretty  adventurous for a coward the way he runs  of his arms aloft the way he swings  across platforms he does this things  with a sense of determination and  derring-do he's basically the little  engine that could and it's genuinely  hard to resist how charming he is the  more you control him the more he wins  you over unravels developer a  sweden-based studio called cold would  interactive has put yarny at the center  of the games publicity campaign and that  definitely makes sense I asked five  other people at gamespot to tell me the  first thing that came to mind when they  thought of unravel and the majority  responses was how cute and charming yani  is I recently got to play the games  first two levels at preview event hosted  by its publisher EA and i have to say  going in I was a bit worried it would be  a familiar story you know was sort of  dainty and thuy in the game that had no  real substance beyond this but from what  I played I was particularly impressed by  two things festival let's talk about the  world of unravel yani was born as a ball  of red yarn that one night fell out of  his owners basket as she walked upstairs  for bed it's a very tell like set up  similar to Toy Story you know things  that magically come to life when humans  are out of sight what makes it work so  well is how much detail and dedication  has gone into designing the world that  surrounds yani unravel presents this  eight  ception alee detailed depiction of life  just that ankle heights rugs become  massive stretches of carpet the size of  football fields kitchen tables are these  onimous wooden stadiums something as  simple as a concrete step can become  like a boulder that's very hard to climb  over if you ever looked under your bed  or sofa and suddenly found yourself  gazing at how different the world looks  from floor level then you're going to  really enjoy exploring unravels world  that leads me to the second thing from  what I've played so far unravel is a  finely tuned puzzle platformer that  creates very smart challenges from  everyday items you honestly won't  believe how much thought has to go into  opening a shed door if you're only eight  inches tall let alone climbing over a  garden fence as Jonnie moves the twine  he's made of unravels a bit like a trail  of breadcrumbs so if you walk too far  without finding any more twine he's  reduced to this gaunt wireframe figure  and he can't walk any further this means  you need to explore the world to find  hidden pieces of wool and you have to  actually figure out how to collect yarn  that seems a bit out of reach but that  weakness of the irony is also his  strength he can also use his wall to do  a manner of different things such as  abseil down giant drops or swing across  platforms Indiana Jones style he can  also fast in his yarn on two knots of  wolver scattered throughout each level  which sort of helped him create a whole  manner of tools from rope bridges to  makeshift trampolines unravels  challenges encourage this kind of  experimentation of mixing different  tools together actually there's there's  also a third thing the music unravels  creative director Martin salen believes  that for a game of zero dialogue music  is one of its most important  storytelling tools music is the voice of  the game he says the soundtrack was the  first thing in unravel that I totally  fell for it's a kingly melancholic at  times it's charming and relaxing others  it works beautifully for a game laced  with nostalgia as an air of sadness and  mystery and that works ideally for the  kind of story that unravel is trying to  tell having played the first two hours  of unravel signs are positive so far it  may star in a door  homemade creature of wire and wool but  behind its charming exterior is a  challenging puzzle platformer that seems  to have all the fundamentals in the  right place gamespot's review of unravel  will go live monday februari 8th a day  before the game's global release  you
 

